losin-containing protein (VCP)/p97\nLeishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-21T12:01:08.48Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7p4j2525rj","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7p4j2525rj"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ypfc848m8z","title":"LINF_230018800","description":"small hydrophilic endoplasmic reticulum-associated protein (sherp) (SHERP2)\nLeishmania infantum (strain JPCM5)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-17T09:06:49.738Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ypfc848m8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ypfc848m8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"w3j28rfkzs","title":"The endoplasmic reticulum protein Erg28 restrains Mto1–Mto2–γ-TuSC–mediated microtubule assembly","description":"Western blotting raw dataset for the published work: \"The endoplasmic reticulum protein Erg28 restrains Mto1–Mto2–γ-TuSC–mediated microtubule assembly\".","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-14T13:11:05.998Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/w3j28rfkzs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/w3j28rfkzs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuanhai","last_name":"Fu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microtubule"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bkxfv6yzn5","title":"ARMH3-mediated recruitment of Phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta directs Golgi-to-endosome trafficking and activation of the antiviral effector STING","description":"The cGAS-STING pathway mediates cytoplasmic DNA-triggered innate immunity. STING activation is initiated by cyclic-GMP-AMP (cGAMP)-induced translocation from the endoplasmic reticulum and sulfated glycosaminoglycans-induced polymerization at the Golgi. Here we examined the mechanisms underlying STING transport and activation beyond the Golgi. A genome-wide CRISPR–Cas9 screen identified Armadillo-like helical domain-containing protein 3 (ARMH3) as critical for STING activation. Upon cGAMP-triggered translocation, ARMH3 interacted with STING at the Golgi and recruited phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta (PI4KB) to synthesize PI4P, which directed STING Golgi-to-endosome trafficking via PI4P-binding proteins AP-1 and GGA2. Disrupting PI4P-dependent lipid transport through RNAi of other PI4P-binding proteins impaired STING activation. Consistently, disturbed lipid composition inhibited STING activation whereas aberrantly elevated cellular PI4P led to cGAS-independent STING activation. Armh3fl/fllLyzCre/Cre mice were susceptible to DNA virus challenge in vivo. Thus, ARMH3 bridges STING and PIK4B to generate PI4P for STING transportation and activation, an interaction conserved in all eukaryotes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-20T13:53:07.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkxfv6yzn5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkxfv6yzn5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"run","last_name":"fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinying","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Cytokines","Phosphoinositides","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6w84hgr7w2","title":"TAK1 is an essential kinase for STING trafficking,Ma et al.","description":"The translocation of stimulator of interferon genes (STING) from the endoplasmic reticulum (ER) to the ER–Golgi intermediate compartment (ERGIC) enables its activation. However, the mechanism underlying the regulation of STING exiting from the ER remains elusive. Here, we found that STING induces the activation of transforming growth factor beta-activated kinase 1 (TAK1) prior to STING trafficking in a TAK1 binding protein 1 (TAB1)-dependent manner. Intriguingly, activated TAK1 directly mediates STING phosphorylation on serine 355, which facilitates its interaction with STING ER exit protein (STEEP) and thereby promotes its oligomerization and translocation to the ERGIC for subsequent activation. Importantly, activation of TAK1 by monophosphoryl lipid A, a TLR4 agonist, boosts cGAMP-induced antitumor immunity dependent on STING phosphorylation in a mouse allograft tumor model. Taken together, TAK1 was identified as a checkpoint for STING activation by promoting its trafficking, providing a basis for combinatory tumor immunotherapy and intervention in STING-related diseases.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-15T05:34:13.759Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6w84hgr7w2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6w84hgr7w2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"mingtong","last_name":"ma","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mgwwth625t","title":"LDHU3_16.1950","description":"Endoplasmic reticulum oxidoreductin; Leishmania donovani (HU3 strain)","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-04T18:03:56.988Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mgwwth625t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mgwwth625t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose M.","last_name":"Requena","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","grant_id":"PID2020-117916RB-I00/AEI/10.13039/501100011033"}],"categories":["Gene","Protein","Leishmania"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"tr4wzcp7h4","title":"A Study of Mechanism of RACK1-dependent ZAKα activation at stalled and collided ribosomes. Vind et al.","description":"Despite a growing interest in the ribotoxic stress response (RSR), it remains unknown how the upstream p38 and JNK-activating MAP3 kinase ZAKa senses translational impairment. Combining Alphafold3 prediction and RNA crosslinking and immunoprecipitation (CLIP), we uncover that ZAKa dynamically monitors the mRNA exit channel of elongating ribosomes. This is accomplished by ZAKa via direct interactions with the ribosomal proteins RACK1 and RPS27 as well as 18S rRNA helix-26. In this conformation, the RNA-binding S (sensing) and C-terminal domain of ZAKa span across the path of ribosome-exiting mRNA. Progressive elongation effectively threads ZAKa off the ribosome, while mRNA stasis stabilizes the interaction allowing for kinase activation. Prolonged binding of ZAKa to slow-elongating, stalled and collided ribosomes is associated with sequestration of the SAM domain on RACK1, allowing for transient ZAKa dimerization, activation loop trans-autophosphorylation and RSR activation. Our findings highlight how ZAKa senses both stalled and collided ribosomes through overlapping mechanisms.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-01T16:12:44.382Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/tr4wzcp7h4","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/tr4wzcp7h4"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Anna Constance ","last_name":"Vind","orcid":"0000-0001-5074-2713"},{"first_name":"José Fransisco ","last_name":"Martinez Hernandez ","orcid":"0000-0002-3457-2088"},{"first_name":"Zhenzhen","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kelly","last_name":" Mordente ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sofia ","last_name":"Ramalho ","orcid":"0000-0002-9091-2657"},{"first_name":"Catarina","last_name":"Pechincha ","orcid":"0000-0002-4650-4250"},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Ryder","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melanie","last_name":"Blasius","orcid":"0000-0001-5032-2209"},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Bekker-Jensen","orcid":"0000-0002-7308-4597"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Signaling","Ribosome"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":128863,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":146,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":128863,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":128517,"restricted":61,"embargoed":285},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":3470},{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2168},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":618},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":465},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":403},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":388},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":358},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":303},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":266}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":126263},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1896},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":183},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research D